{'chapter_id': 3, 'bundle_token': 'b1-0c96ed4e', 'content': 'The Ring of Nestor
is described by the National Archaeological Museum as a gold signet ring with a
hoop diameter of about 1.9 cm and an oval bezel measuring approximately 2 × 3.4
cm.[3] The ring is assigned to the Mycenaean period, with a stylistic date in 
the 16th–15th century BC, and belongs to the museum’s Collection of Prehistoric
Antiquities under inventory number P 6209.[3] Comparative measurements of rings
from different periods presented by Evans, including medieval, Renaissance, 
Roman and Greek examples, indicate that the inner diameter of the Ring of 
Nestor falls within the range documented for adult wearers, although Evans did 
not specify a particular wearer’s gender or status for this object.[2]\n\nThe 
engraved bezel shows a multi‑figure scene that has attracted attention for its 
narrative complexity.[3] According to the museum’s description, the composition
is organised around two couples. On the left, a man and woman stand at the 
entrance of a building; on the right, a second couple stands on a shoreline 
indicated by rocks and an oared vessel.[3] The figures wear garments typical of
Mycenaean and Minoan dress: the women in long pleated skirts and the men in the
Minoan loincloth or *zoma*, with swords and spears hanging at their waists.[3] 
The museum notes that the gestures of the left‑hand couple, which are not 
easily visible to the naked eye, suggest a close relationship, with the woman 
wrapping her fingers around the man’s arm as if drawing him nearer while he 
extends his free hand towards her face.[3]\n\nThe ring functions as a signet, 
with the engraved design intended to be impressed into a softer material such 
as clay or wax.[2] Evans observed in his discussion of the Tiryns Hoard that 
differences could sometimes be detected between the engraving on a signet and 
its impression, noting for another gold signet that a figure who holds a dagger
in one hand on the ring appears to use the opposite hand in the impression.[2] 
Although the surviving excerpts do not describe such a discrepancy for the Ring
of Nestor itself, the comment illustrates the technical care required in 
reading Aegean glyptic images from both objects and their impressions. The 
National Archaeological Museum situates the ring within a broader corpus of 
Mycenaean signet rings in which scenes rarely depict everyday life but instead 
focus on symbolic moments drawn from religious or mythological spheres.[3]', 
