'b5-764586fe', 'content': 'Questions about the authenticity of the Ring of 
Nestor emerged in 20th‑century scholarship, in part because of its complex 
acquisition history and in part because of broader concerns about restorations 
and forgeries associated with Bronze Age Aegean art.[2][4] The hoard that 
included the ring was discovered outside official excavation channels during 
wartime, and Evans’s account relies on information passed through 
intermediaries rather than on direct stratigraphic observation.[2] At the same 
time, research on the work of Emile Gilliéron and his son, who served as 
official artists for Greek museums and collaborated closely with Heinrich 
Schliemann and Arthur Evans, has drawn attention to the extent of restoration 
and reconstruction in early presentations of Minoan and Mycenaean material.[4] 
A perspective essay by the Metropolitan Museum of Art notes that the 
Gilliérons’ reconstructions at Knossos and elsewhere sometimes incorporated 
imaginative elements, such as misidentified figures and invented border 
patterns, and that scholars have long debated the validity of these 
restorations.[4]\n\nIn this context, Ingo Pini’s 1998 article in the *Oxford 
Journal of Archaeology*, as summarised in an online description, examined the 
authenticity of the Ring of Nestor by focusing on its iconography and on the 
possibility that it might be a forgery produced within the Gilliéron circle.[5]
The summary states that Pini reviewed arguments that the ring’s imagery could 
reflect cultural practices and artistic conventions from the 1920s rather than 
the Late Bronze Age, and that he weighed these concerns against comparisons 
with securely provenanced Minoan–Mycenaean signet rings.[5] The same 
description indicates that new technical observations presented in the article 
support the ring’s authenticity, countering earlier doubts, and that particular
stylistic and manufacturing details of the Ring of Nestor align with those of 
established Bronze Age artefacts.[5]\n\nTheodore Eliopoulos’s 2013 study of the
ring’s iconography, published in the *Annuario della Scuola Archeologica di 
Atene e delle Missioni Italiane in Oriente*, also engages with the authenticity
debate.[1] In the abstract of his article, Eliopoulos states that he reassessed
key iconographic features of the Ring of Nestor within the wider context of 
Aegean forgeries, drawing on historical scholarship and recent discoveries in 
order to question and affirm previous conclusions.[1] An online set of key 
points associated with the article summarises his position by noting that 
contradictions in technical analyses of Aegean objects have heightened the 
importance of detailed iconographic study for evaluating both authenticity and 
cultural significance.[1] Together, the studies by Pini and Eliopoulos present 
a picture in which the Ring of Nestor is treated as a problematic but, in their
view, ultimately defensible artefact, whose imagery and manufacture are 
considered consistent with its proposed Late Bronze Age date while still 
serving as a test case for methods of detecting forgeries in Aegean 
archaeology.[1][5]', 'evidence_refs': ['e5', 'e6', 'e34', 'e35', 'e36', 'e37', 
