{'chapter_id': 6, 'bundle_token': 'b6-424088e3', 'content': 'The Ring of Nestor
has held a sustained place in scholarship on Aegean Bronze Age religion and 
glyptic art. Arthur Evans’s early presentation of the Tiryns Hoard in the 
*Journal of Hellenic Studies* framed the ring as a key example of gold signet 
rings and bead seals from a Mycenaean rock‑cut tomb near Thisbê in Boeotia, 
which he described as a “royal treasure.”[2] Evans compared elements of the 
hoard’s jewellery and glass‑paste ornaments with other Late Minoan III B 
assemblages, contributing to the development of relative chronologies for 
Mycenaean and Minoan material culture.[2] The ring later entered the National 
Archaeological Museum in Athens, where it is exhibited in the Collection of 
Prehistoric Antiquities; the museum’s presentation emphasises both its 
provenance from the Tiryns Hoard and the narrative character of its engraved 
scene.[3]\n\nMuseum interpretation of the ring’s imagery situates it within 
broader observations about Mycenaean art. The National Archaeological Museum 
notes that Mycenaean art between about 1600 and 1100 BC generally avoided 
direct illustration of everyday life, instead favouring moments and symbols 
from the realm of religion, and that explicit expressions of emotion are rare, 
with hints of affection more often confined to depictions of animals and their 
offspring on signet rings.[3] In this context, the museum describes the Ring of
Nestor as an important example because its composition of two couples, one at a
building entrance and another on a shoreline marked by rocks and a ship, has 
been interpreted as a narrative with mythological overtones.[3] The ship, with 
a high stern and a cabin resembling the Flotilla Frieze from Akrotiri on Thera,
connects the ring to discussions of seafaring and coastal iconography in Aegean
art.[3]\n\nThe ring’s scene has invited more specific identifications. The 
National Archaeological Museum reports that the couple, possibly the same pair 
shown in two successive moments, has been identified by some interpreters with 
Theseus and Ariadne during their escape to Athens following the killing of the 
Minotaur, while other readings treat the composition as a farewell between men 
departing for battle and their partners, comparable to imagery on the later 
Warrior Krater from Mycenae.[3] In either case, scholars cited in the museum’s 
commentary see the ring as contributing to debates about the presence or 
absence of love and emotional relationships in the visual record of the 
Mycenaean period, given that the figures’ gestures suggest intimacy even though
their facial expressions are rendered in an abstract manner.[3]\n\nIn addition 
to its iconographic significance, the Ring of Nestor has become a case study in
the history of archaeological restoration and the assessment of authenticity in
Aegean studies. The Metropolitan Museum of Art’s discussion of Emile Gilliéron 
and his son highlights both their influential role as official artists for 
Greek museums and the subsequent debates over the accuracy of their 
reconstructions, such as the reinterpretation of the “Saffron‑Gatherer” fresco 
as depicting a monkey rather than a boy and the questioning of modern additions
in the “Bull‑Leapers” fresco and the “Priest‑King” relief.[4] Against this 
background, modern analyses like those of Pini and Eliopoulos use the Ring of 
Nestor to examine how stylistic comparison, technical observation and knowledge
of early 20th‑century restoration practices can be combined to distinguish 
authentic Bronze Age artefacts from potential forgeries.[1][4][5]', 
