{'chapter_id': 2, 'bundle_token': 'b2-b56280ac', 'content': 'The first detailed
account of the Ring of Nestor appeared in a study of a gold treasure from a 
Mycenaean rock‑cut chamber tomb near the site of Thisbê in Boeotia, described 
by British archaeologist Arthur Evans in the *Journal of Hellenic Studies*.[2] 
Evans reported that the hoard, which he called a “royal treasure” of gold rings
and bead‑seals, was found in 1915 during wartime conditions that diverted 
discoveries from official archaeological channels.[2] According to information 
that Evans attributed to William A. Heurtley of the British School at Athens, 
the discovery was made by a local peasant in a rock‑cut tomb near the village 
of Dombrena, on the acropolis height of Thisbê; Heurtley in turn recorded that 
he had seen three chamber tombs with access dromoi in an olive grove near this 
spot.[2] The assemblage from these tombs included bronze spearheads, a short 
sword of late Mycenaean type, a bronze razor of advanced form, and an iron 
double‑axe head, as well as glass‑paste beads and gold‑foil covered pendants 
that Evans and Alan Wace dated to early Late Minoan III B on typological 
grounds.[2]\n\nIn the catalogue of the National Archaeological Museum in 
Athens, the Ring of Nestor is listed as a gold signet ring in the Collection of
Prehistoric Antiquities, with inventory number P 6209, and its provenance is 
given as the Tiryns Hoard from Tiryns in the Argolid.[3] The museum description
dates the ring broadly to the 16th–15th century BC, within the Mycenaean 
period, and records that it is displayed in the Exhibition of Prehistoric 
Antiquities, Gallery 4, showcase M28.[3] The combination of Evans’s early 
report of a hoard near Thisbê and the museum’s record of provenance from Tiryns
reflects the complex history of acquisition and classification of the 
assemblage, which passed through unofficial channels before entering 
institutional collections.[2][3]\n\nLater scholarship on the Ring of Nestor has
situated it within wider debates about the circulation of artefacts and the 
reliability of provenances for objects acquired during wartime or through the 
art market. Theodore Eliopoulos, in a 2013 article in the *Annuario della 
Scuola Archeologica di Atene e delle Missioni Italiane in Oriente*, analysed 
the ring’s iconography in connection with broader discussions of Aegean 
forgeries and authenticity, drawing on earlier scholarship and more recent 
discoveries to reassess its motifs and implications.[1] The same study, as 
summarised in an online abstract, emphasises that contradictions in technical 
analyses of Aegean artefacts have increased the importance of detailed 
iconographic comparison when evaluating both the cultural significance and the 
authenticity of objects such as the Ring of Nestor.[1]', 'evidence_refs': 
