Heurtley in turn recorded that he had seen three chamber tombs with access 
dromoi in an olive grove near this spot.[2] The assemblage from these tombs 
included bronze spearheads, a short sword of late Mycenaean type, a bronze 
razor of advanced form, and an iron double‑axe head, as well as glass‑paste 
beads and gold‑foil covered pendants that Evans and Alan Wace dated to early 
Late Minoan III B on typological grounds.[2]\n\nIn the catalogue of the 
National Archaeological Museum in Athens, the Ring of Nestor is listed as a 
gold signet ring in the Collection of Prehistoric Antiquities, with inventory 
number P 6209, and its provenance is given as the Tiryns Hoard from Tiryns in 
the Argolid.[3] The museum description dates the ring broadly to the 16th–15th 
century BC, within the Mycenaean period, and records that it is displayed in 
the Exhibition of Prehistoric Antiquities, Gallery 4, showcase M28.[3] The 
combination of Evans’s early report of a hoard near Thisbê and the museum’s 
record of provenance from Tiryns reflects the complex history of acquisition 
and classification of the assemblage, which passed through unofficial channels 
before entering institutional collections.[2][3]\n\nLater scholarship on the 
Ring of Nestor has situated it within wider debates about the circulation of 
artefacts and the reliability of provenances for objects acquired during 
wartime or through the art market. Theodore Eliopoulos, in a 2013 article in 
the *Annuario della Scuola Archeologica di Atene e delle Missioni Italiane in 
Oriente*, analysed the ring’s iconography in connection with broader 
discussions of Aegean forgeries and authenticity, drawing on earlier 
scholarship and more recent discoveries to reassess its motifs and 
implications.[1] The same study, as summarised in an online abstract, 
emphasises that contradictions in technical analyses of Aegean artefacts have 
increased the importance of detailed iconographic comparison when evaluating 
both the cultural significance and the authenticity of objects such as the Ring
of Nestor.[1]\n\n## Description\n\nThe Ring of Nestor is described by the 
National Archaeological Museum in Athens as a Mycenaean gold signet ring 
belonging to the Collection of Prehistoric Antiquities, inventory number P 
6209.[3] The museum records a hoop diameter of about 1.9 cm and an oval bezel 
measuring approximately 2 × 3.4 cm, with a stylistic date in the 16th–15th 
century BC.[3] Arthur Evans, in his study of the Tiryns Hoard, compared ring 
diameters from medieval, Renaissance, Roman and Greek material and reported 
average inner diameters between about 17 and 19 mm for adult wearers, a range 
within which the Ring of Nestor falls, although he did not infer a specific 
wearer’s gender or status for this particular object.[2]\n\nThe engraved bezel 
carries a multi‑figure composition that has drawn attention for its apparent 
narrative character.[3] According to the museum’s description, the scene is 
organised around two couples. On the left, a man and woman stand at the 
entrance of a building; on the right, a second couple appears on a beach 
indicated by rocks and an oared vessel.[3] The figures wear garments typical of
Aegean Bronze Age dress: the women are shown in long, pleated skirts, and the 
men in the Minoan loincloth (*zoma*), with swords and spears suspended at their
waists.[3] The museum remarks that the relationship between the left‑hand 
couple is suggested by their gestures, which are difficult to see without 
magnification: the woman embraces the man’s arm, wrapping her fingers around it
as if pulling him closer, while the man reaches his free hand toward her 
face.[3]\n\nAs a signet ring, the object was designed to produce impressions of
its image in softer materials such as clay or wax.[2] In his discussion of the 
Tiryns Hoard, Evans noted that differences could sometimes be observed between 
the engraving on a ring and its impression, citing an example in which a figure
uses a dagger with one hand on the signet but appears to use the opposite hand 
in the impressed design.[2] The surviving excerpts from his article do not 
mention such a discrepancy for the Ring of Nestor itself, but his comments 
illustrate the care required when interpreting Aegean glyptic imagery from both
engraved surfaces and their impressions.[2] The National Archaeological Museum 
situates the Ring of Nestor within a broader corpus of Mycenaean signet rings 
in which scenes rarely portray everyday activities; the museum’s overview of 
Mycenaean art between about 1600 and 1100 BC emphasises that artists generally 
focused on moments and symbols from religious spheres and that explicit 
emotional expression was limited, with signs of affection more commonly 
appearing in depictions of animals and their offspring than in human 
scenes.[3]\n\n## Iconography and 
interpretations\n\n暂无可核实的补充资料。\n\n## Authenticity 
debate\n\nQuestions about the authenticity of the Ring of Nestor emerged in 
20th‑century scholarship, in part because of its complex acquisition history 
and in part because of broader concerns about restorations and forgeries 
associated with Bronze Age Aegean art.[2][4] The hoard that included the ring 
was discovered outside official excavation channels during wartime, and Evans’s
account relies on information passed through intermediaries rather than on 
direct stratigraphic observation.[2] At the same time, research on the work of 
Emile Gilliéron and his son, who served as official artists for Greek museums 
and collaborated closely with Heinrich Schliemann and Arthur Evans, has drawn 
attention to the extent of restoration and reconstruction in early 
presentations of Minoan and Mycenaean material.[4] A perspective essay by the 
Metropolitan Museum of Art notes that the Gilliérons’ reconstructions at 
Knossos and elsewhere sometimes incorporated imaginative elements, such as 
misidentified figures and invented border patterns, and that scholars have long
debated the validity of these restorations.[4]\n\nIn this context, Ingo Pini’s 
1998 article in the *Oxford Journal of Archaeology*, as summarised in an online
description, examined the authenticity of the Ring of Nestor by focusing on its
iconography and on the possibility that it might be a forgery produced within 
the Gilliéron circle.[5] The summary states that Pini reviewed arguments that 
the ring’s imagery could reflect cultural practices and artistic conventions 
from the 1920s rather than the Late Bronze Age, and that he weighed these 
concerns against comparisons with securely provenanced Minoan–Mycenaean signet 
rings.[5] The same description indicates that new technical observations 
presented in the article support the ring’s authenticity, countering earlier 
doubts, and that particular stylistic and manufacturing details of the Ring of 
Nestor align with those of established Bronze Age artefacts.[5]\n\nTheodore 
Eliopoulos’s 2013 study of the ring’s iconography, published in the *Annuario 
della Scuola Archeologica di Atene e delle Missioni Italiane in Oriente*, also 
engages with the authenticity debate.[1] In the abstract of his article, 
Eliopoulos states that he reassessed key iconographic features of the Ring of 
Nestor within the wider context of Aegean forgeries, drawing on historical 
scholarship and recent discoveries in order to question and affirm previous 
conclusions.[1] An online set of key points associated with the article 
summarises his position by noting that contradictions in technical analyses of 
Aegean objects have heightened the importance of detailed iconographic study 
for evaluating both authenticity and cultural significance.[1] Together, the 
studies by Pini and Eliopoulos present a picture in which the Ring of Nestor is
treated as a problematic but, in their view, ultimately defensible artefact, 
whose imagery and manufacture are considered consistent with its proposed Late 
Bronze Age date while still serving as a test case for methods of detecting 
forgeries in Aegean archaeology.[1][5]\n\n## Later reception and scholarly 
significance\n\nThe Ring of Nestor has maintained a significant place in 
scholarship on Aegean Bronze Age religion and glyptic art. Arthur Evans’s 
presentation of the Tiryns Hoard in the *Journal of Hellenic Studies* 
introduced the ring as part of a “royal treasure” of gold rings and bead‑seals 
from a Mycenaean rock‑cut chamber tomb near Thisbê in Boeotia, and used the 
associated glass‑paste ornaments and gold jewellery to refine relative 
chronologies for Late Minoan III B material.[2] The ring later entered the 
National Archaeological Museum in Athens, where it is catalogued in the 
Collection of Prehistoric Antiquities (inv. no. P 6209) and exhibited in the 
Exhibition of Prehistoric Antiquities, Gallery 4, showcase M28.[3] The museum’s
entry highlights both its provenance from the Tiryns Hoard and the narrative 
character of its engraved scene.[3]\n\nIn its broader commentary on Mycenaean 
art from about 1600 to 1100 BC, the National Archaeological Museum observes 
that artistic representations rarely depict everyday life, instead 
concentrating on moments and symbols related to religion.[3] The museum notes 
that, apart from occasional smiles in wall paintings, emotional expressions are
generally omitted, and that scenes suggesting affection are mainly found on 
signet rings, often between animals and their young.[3] Against this 
background, the Ring of Nestor is described as an important example because its
scene of two couples—one at a building entrance and another on a shoreline 
marked by rocks and an oared ship—has been treated as a narrative composition 
with possible mythological resonance.[3] The ship, with high stern and cabin 
reminiscent of the Flotilla Frieze from Akrotiri on Thera, has linked the ring 
to discussions of maritime imagery and coastal landscapes in Aegean 
iconography.[3]\n\nThe same museum commentary reports more specific 
interpretive proposals for the scene. One line of interpretation identifies the
couple, perhaps shown in two successive moments, with Theseus and Ariadne 
during their escape to Athens after the killing of the Minotaur, while another 
regards the composition as a farewell between men departing for battle and 
their partners, analogous to imagery on the later Warrior Krater from 
Mycenae.[3] Although these readings differ, both treat the ring as relevant to 
debates about the representation of love and emotional relationships in the 
visual record of the Mycenaean period, since the figures’ gestures suggest 
intimacy despite the abstract rendering of their faces.[3]\n\nBeyond its 
iconographic interest, the Ring of Nestor has become a case study in the 
history of archaeological restoration and in approaches to authenticity in 
Aegean studies. A perspective essay by the Metropolitan Museum of Art on 
historic images of the Greek Bronze Age discusses the work of Emile Gilliéron 
and his son, who served for twenty‑five years as official artists for Greek 
museums and collaborated closely with Heinrich Schliemann and Evans.[4] The 
essay notes that their reconstructions at Knossos and elsewhere sometimes 
incorporated imaginative elements, such as interpreting the “Saffron‑Gatherer” 
fresco as a boy rather than a monkey and adding rock‑pattern borders to the 
“Bull‑Leapers” fresco without archaeological support.[4] It also records that 
scholars have long debated the validity of Gilliéron restorations and, in the 
case of the “Priest‑King” relief, have produced alternative reconstructions 
from the same fragments.[4] Modern analyses of the Ring of Nestor, including 
work by Theodore Eliopoulos on its iconography and by Ingo Pini on its 
authenticity, draw on this background to explore how stylistic comparison, 
technical observation and awareness of early 20th‑century restoration practices
can be combined to distinguish genuine Bronze Age artefacts from potential 
forgeries in the Aegean corpus.[1][2][4]\n\n## References\n\n[1] (PDF) SOME 
