'b6-424088e3', 'content': 'The Ring of Nestor is frequently discussed in 
studies of Aegean Bronze Age religion and glyptic art.[1][2][3] Arthur Evans’s 
presentation of the Tiryns Hoard in the *Journal of Hellenic Studies*, 
published in the early 20th century, introduced the ring as part of a “royal 
treasure” of gold rings and bead‑seals from a Mycenaean rock‑cut chamber tomb 
near Thisbê in Boeotia and used the associated glass‑paste ornaments, faience 
plaques and jewellery to refine relative chronologies for Late Minoan III B 
material.[2] The ring later entered the National Archaeological Museum in 
Athens, where it is catalogued in the Collection of Prehistoric Antiquities 
(inv. no. P 6209) and exhibited in the Exhibition of Prehistoric Antiquities, 
Gallery 4, showcase M28; the museum’s entry draws attention to its provenance 
from the Tiryns Hoard and to the narrative character of its engraved 
scene.[3]\n\nIn its overview of Mycenaean art from about 1600 to 1100 BC, the 
National Archaeological Museum states that representations rarely depict 
everyday life and instead focus on moments and symbols from the realm of 
religion, and it remarks that explicit emotional expression is generally 
avoided, with indications of affection more commonly found on signet rings in 
scenes involving animals and their young.[3] Within this framework, the museum 
presentation of the Ring of Nestor emphasises the composition of two 
couples—one at a building entrance and another on a shoreline marked by rocks 
and an oared ship—and the way in which the gestures of the left‑hand couple 
suggest a close relationship despite the abstraction of their faces.[3] The 
museum reports that some interpreters have identified the couple, possibly 
shown in two successive moments, with Theseus and Ariadne in the course of 
their escape to Athens after the killing of the Minotaur, linking the ship on 
the ring to the vessel imagery in the Flotilla Frieze from Akrotiri on Thera 
dated to the 16th century BC.[3] Other interpreters, also cited by the museum, 
regard the scene as a farewell between men departing for battle and their 
partners, comparable to the composition on the later Warrior Krater from 
Mycenae.[3]\n\nModern scholarship on the Ring of Nestor has likewise used it as
a case study in the assessment of authenticity and the history of 
archaeological reconstruction. The Metropolitan Museum of Art’s essay on 
historic images of the Greek Bronze Age discusses the role of Emile Gilliéron 
and his son as official artists for Greek museums and notes that their 
reconstructions at Knossos and other sites sometimes introduced elements not 
securely based on the archaeological record, such as the interpretation of the 
“Saffron‑Gatherer” fresco as a boy rather than a monkey and the addition of 
rock‑pattern borders to the “Bull‑Leapers” fresco.[4] The essay points out that
scholars have long debated these reconstructions and, in the case of the 
“Priest‑King” relief, have proposed multiple alternative compositions from the 
same fragments.[4] Against this background, studies by Ingo Pini and Theodore 
Eliopoulos use the Ring of Nestor to explore how stylistic comparison, 
technical observation and knowledge of early 20th‑century restoration practices
can be combined to distinguish authentic Bronze Age artefacts from possible 
forgeries in the Aegean corpus, with both authors arguing that the ring’s 
iconography and manufacture can be understood within Late Bronze Age religious 
and artistic traditions.[1][2][4][5]', 'evidence_refs': ['e5', 'e8', 'e9', 
