"severity": "hard", "report": "# Ring of Nestor\n\nThe **Ring of Nestor** is a 
Mycenaean gold signet ring dating to the 16th–15th century BC and now kept in 
the National Archaeological Museum in Athens, within the Collection of 
Prehistoric Antiquities (inv. no. P 6209).[3] The ring forms part of the 
so‑called Tiryns Hoard, a group of gold rings and bead seals reportedly 
discovered in 1915 in a rock‑cut chamber tomb near Thisbê in Boeotia, and later
associated with the site of Tiryns in the Argolid.[2][3] Its oval bezel, about 
2 × 3.4 cm in size with a hoop diameter around 1.9 cm, bears an engraved scene 
showing two couples, one at the entrance of a building and another on a rocky 
shoreline marked by an oared ship.[3]\n\nSince its first detailed publication 
in the early 20th century, the Ring of Nestor has played a role in discussions 
of Mycenaean and Minoan religion, glyptic art and narrative imagery.[2] The 
scene has been interpreted either as a mythological episode, potentially 
involving heroes such as Theseus and Ariadne, or as a farewell before departure
for war, and has therefore been used to explore the representation of affection
and emotion in Bronze Age Aegean art.[3] The ring has also stood at the centre 
of a long‑running authenticity debate. Scholars such as Ingo Pini have examined
whether it is a genuine Late Bronze Age object or a modern forgery connected 
with the circle of Emile Gilliéron, while other studies, including work by 
Theodore Eliopoulos and technical observations reported in the late 20th 
century, argue that its manufacture and iconography are consistent with 
Minoan–Mycenaean signet rings.[1][4][5]\n\n## Discovery and provenance\n\nThe 
first detailed account of the Ring of Nestor appeared in a study of a gold 
treasure from a Mycenaean rock‑cut chamber tomb near the site of Thisbê in 
Boeotia, described by British archaeologist Arthur Evans in the *Journal of 
Hellenic Studies*.[2] Evans reported that the hoard, which he called a “royal 
treasure” of gold rings and bead‑seals, was found in 1915 during wartime 
conditions that diverted discoveries from official archaeological channels.[2] 
According to information that Evans attributed to William A. Heurtley of the 
British School at Athens, the discovery was made by a local peasant in a 
rock‑cut tomb near the village of Dombrena, on the acropolis height of Thisbê; 
Heurtley in turn recorded that he had seen three chamber tombs with access 
dromoi in an olive grove near this spot.[2] The assemblage from these tombs 
included bronze spearheads, a short sword of late Mycenaean type, a bronze 
razor of advanced form, and an iron double‑axe head, as well as glass‑paste 
beads and gold‑foil covered pendants that Evans and Alan Wace dated to early 
Late Minoan III B on typological grounds.[2]\n\nIn the catalogue of the 
National Archaeological Museum in Athens, the Ring of Nestor is listed as a 
gold signet ring in the Collection of Prehistoric Antiquities, with inventory 
number P 6209, and its provenance is given as the Tiryns Hoard from Tiryns in 
the Argolid.[3] The museum description dates the ring broadly to the 16th–15th 
century BC, within the Mycenaean period, and records that it is displayed in 
the Exhibition of Prehistoric Antiquities, Gallery 4, showcase M28.[3] The 
combination of Evans’s early report of a hoard near Thisbê and the museum’s 
record of provenance from Tiryns reflects the complex history of acquisition 
and classification of the assemblage, which passed through unofficial channels 
before entering institutional collections.[2][3]\n\nLater scholarship on the 
Ring of Nestor has situated it within wider debates about the circulation of 
artefacts and the reliability of provenances for objects acquired during 
wartime or through the art market. Theodore Eliopoulos, in a 2013 article in 
the *Annuario della Scuola Archeologica di Atene e delle Missioni Italiane in 
Oriente*, analysed the ring’s iconography in connection with broader 
discussions of Aegean forgeries and authenticity, drawing on earlier 
scholarship and more recent discoveries to reassess its motifs and 
implications.[1] The same study, as summarised in an online abstract, 
emphasises that contradictions in technical analyses of Aegean artefacts have 
increased the importance of detailed iconographic comparison when evaluating 
both the cultural significance and the authenticity of objects such as the Ring
of Nestor.[1]\n\n## Description\n\nThe Ring of Nestor is described by the 
National Archaeological Museum in Athens as a Mycenaean gold signet ring 
belonging to the Collection of Prehistoric Antiquities, inventory number P 
6209.[3] The museum records a hoop diameter of about 1.9 cm and an oval bezel 
measuring approximately 2 × 3.4 cm, with a stylistic date in the 16th–15th 
century BC.[3] Arthur Evans, in his study of the Tiryns Hoard, compared ring 
diameters from medieval, Renaissance, Roman and Greek material and reported 
average inner diameters between about 17 and 19 mm for adult wearers, a range 
within which the Ring of Nestor falls, although he did not infer a specific 
wearer’s gender or status for this particular object.[2]\n\nThe engraved bezel 
carries a multi‑figure composition that has drawn attention for its apparent 
narrative character.[3] According to the museum’s description, the scene is 
organised around two couples. On the left, a man and woman stand at the 
entrance of a building; on the right, a second couple appears on a beach 
indicated by rocks and an oared vessel.[3] The figures wear garments typical of
Aegean Bronze Age dress: the women are shown in long, pleated skirts, and the 
men in the Minoan loincloth (*zoma*), with swords and spears suspended at their
waists.[3] The museum remarks that the relationship between the left‑hand 
couple is suggested by their gestures, which are difficult to see without 
magnification: the woman embraces the man’s arm, wrapping her fingers around it
as if pulling him closer, while the man reaches his free hand toward her 
face.[3]\n\nAs a signet ring, the object was designed to produce impressions of
its image in softer materials such as clay or wax.[2] In his discussion of the 
Tiryns Hoard, Evans noted that differences could sometimes be observed between 
the engraving on a ring and its impression, citing an example in which a figure
uses a dagger with one hand on the signet but appears to use the opposite hand 
in the impressed design.[2] The surviving excerpts from his article do not 
mention such a discrepancy for the Ring of Nestor itself, but his comments 
illustrate the care required when interpreting Aegean glyptic imagery from both
engraved surfaces and their impressions.[2] The National Archaeological Museum 
situates the Ring of Nestor within a broader corpus of Mycenaean signet rings 
in which scenes rarely portray everyday activities; the museum’s overview of 
Mycenaean art between about 1600 and 1100 BC emphasises that artists generally 
focused on moments and symbols from religious spheres and that explicit 
emotional expression was limited, with signs of affection more commonly 
appearing in depictions of animals and their offspring than in human 
scenes.[3]\n\n## Iconography and interpretations\n\nScholarly interpretations 
of the Ring of Nestor focus on the narrative and symbolic character of the 
engraved scene and its possible place within Aegean Bronze Age religion and 
myth. In his discussion of the Tiryns Hoard, Arthur Evans treated the ring as 
one element within a wider group of signet rings and bead‑seals that he 
associated with Late Minoan III B jewellery assemblages, comparing their motifs
with other Mycenaean and Minoan glyptic material.[2] Evans emphasised the 
presence in the hoard of glass‑paste ornaments, faience plaques with palm 
motifs and perforated necklace elements that he linked to specific 
chronological horizons, thereby situating the ring’s imagery within a broader 
repertoire of Late Bronze Age decorative and religious symbolism.[2]\n\nThe 
National Archaeological Museum in Athens, in its presentation of the ring under
inventory number P 6209, describes the bezel scene as being composed of two 
couples, one at the entrance of a building and the other on a rocky shoreline 
with an oared vessel.[3] The museum commentary states that Mycenaean art 
between about 1600 and 1100 BC tended to avoid direct depictions of everyday 
life and instead concentrated on moments and symbols from the realm of 
religion, and it notes that clear expressions of emotion are rare, with 
indications of affection more often found in images of animals and their young 
than in human figures.[3] On this basis, the museum highlights the Ring of 
Nestor as a case in which a human relationship is conveyed through subtle 
gestures rather than facial expression: the woman of the left‑hand couple is 
described as embracing the man’s arm and pulling him toward her, while the man 
extends his free hand towards her face.[3]\n\nWithin this framework, the museum
cites two main lines of interpretation for the scene. One proposal identifies 
the couple, possibly shown in two successive moments, with Theseus and Ariadne 
during their departure for Athens after the killing of the Minotaur, drawing on
similarities between the ship on the ring—with its high stern and cabin—and the
vessel imagery in the Flotilla Frieze from Akrotiri on Thera dated to the 16th 
century BC.[3] A second proposal reads the composition as a farewell between 
men going to battle and their partners, an interpretation that the museum links
to later martial imagery such as the Warrior Krater from Mycenae.[3] Both 
readings treat the ring’s iconography as contributing to discussions of how 
narrative, mythological references and emotional relationships were expressed 
in Mycenaean glyptic art, while the museum text leaves open which specific myth
or circumstance the image represents.[3]\n\nTechnical observations on Aegean 
glyptic by Evans underscore further aspects of the ring’s iconography. In his 
analysis of signet rings and their impressions, Evans drew attention to the 
possibility of discrepancies between the engraved design and the image produced
in relief, citing an example in which a figure appears to use a dagger with 
different hands on the ring and in its impression.[2] Although this remark 
concerns another object, it has been used in later studies to stress that 
iconographic readings of the Ring of Nestor and comparable signet rings must 
take into account both the engraved surfaces and any surviving impressions, as 
small variations can affect interpretations of gesture, weapon use and 
narrative sequence.[2] More recent work on the ring by Theodore Eliopoulos, as 
outlined in the description of his 2013 article, situates these iconographic 
issues within broader debates about Aegean forgeries and authenticity and 
argues that careful comparison of motifs and scene structures can help clarify 
both the cultural meaning and the genuineness of the Ring of Nestor.[1]\n\n## 
Authenticity debate\n\nQuestions about the authenticity of the Ring of Nestor 
emerged in 20th‑century scholarship, in part because of its complex acquisition
history and in part because of wider concerns about restorations and forgeries 
in Bronze Age Aegean art.[2][4] The hoard that included the ring was discovered
outside official excavation channels during wartime, and Arthur Evans’s account
in the *Journal of Hellenic Studies* relies on information transmitted through 
intermediaries rather than on direct stratigraphic observation of the tomb.[2] 
At the same time, research on the work of Emile Gilliéron and his son, who 
served for twenty‑five years as official artists for Greek museums and 
collaborated closely with Heinrich Schliemann and Evans, has shown that early 
presentations of Minoan and Mycenaean material often involved extensive 
pictorial reconstruction.[4] A perspective essay by the Metropolitan Museum of 
Art records that Gilliéron restorations at Knossos and other sites sometimes 
incorporated imaginative elements, such as interpreting the “Saffron‑Gatherer” 
fresco as a boy rather than a monkey and adding rock‑pattern borders to the 
“Bull‑Leapers” fresco for which there is no archaeological evidence, and notes 
that scholars have long debated the validity of such 
restorations.[4]\n\nAgainst this background, Ingo Pini’s 1998 article in the 
*Oxford Journal of Archaeology*, as described in the summary attached to the 
Scribd copy of the paper, addresses the authenticity of the Ring of Nestor by 
analysing its iconography and considering whether it might be a forgery 
connected with the Gilliéron circle.[5] The summary indicates that Pini 
reviewed arguments that the ring’s imagery had been suspected of reflecting 
cultural practices and artistic conventions of the early 20th century rather 
than the Late Bronze Age, and that he compared the Ring of Nestor with securely
provenanced Minoan–Mycenaean signet rings in order to test these suspicions.[5]
According to the same description, Pini presented new technical 
observations—without detail in the summary—that support the ring’s authenticity
and pointed to stylistic and manufacturing features that he regarded as 
consistent with known Bronze Age artefacts.[5]\n\nTheodore Eliopoulos’s 2013 
study of the ring’s iconography, published in the *Annuario della Scuola 
Archeologica di Atene e delle Missioni Italiane in Oriente*, also engages with 
the authenticity question.[1] The abstract for Eliopoulos’s article explains 
that he analysed key iconographic features of the Ring of Nestor in the context
of Aegean forgeries and authenticity debates, drawing on historical scholarship
and recent discoveries to reconsider previous conclusions.[1] A set of key 
points associated with the paper further states that Eliopoulos discussed 
contradictions in technical analyses of Aegean artefacts and argued that such 
contradictions increased the importance of detailed iconographic comparison 
when evaluating both authenticity and cultural significance.[1] Taken together,
the studies by Pini and Eliopoulos portray the Ring of Nestor as an artefact 
whose genuineness has been questioned but for which recent technical and 
iconographic work has provided arguments in favour of an origin in the Late 
Bronze Age Aegean.[1][5]\n\n## Later reception and scholarly 
significance\n\nThe Ring of Nestor is frequently discussed in studies of Aegean
Bronze Age religion and glyptic art.[1][2][3] Arthur Evans’s presentation of 
the Tiryns Hoard in the *Journal of Hellenic Studies*, published in the early 
20th century, introduced the ring as part of a “royal treasure” of gold rings 
and bead‑seals from a Mycenaean rock‑cut chamber tomb near Thisbê in Boeotia 
and used the associated glass‑paste ornaments, faience plaques and jewellery to
refine relative chronologies for Late Minoan III B material.[2] The ring later 
entered the National Archaeological Museum in Athens, where it is catalogued in
the Collection of Prehistoric Antiquities (inv. no. P 6209) and exhibited in 
the Exhibition of Prehistoric Antiquities, Gallery 4, showcase M28; the 
museum’s entry draws attention to its provenance from the Tiryns Hoard and to 
the narrative character of its engraved scene.[3]\n\nIn its overview of 
Mycenaean art from about 1600 to 1100 BC, the National Archaeological Museum 
states that representations rarely depict everyday life and instead focus on 
moments and symbols from the realm of religion, and it remarks that explicit 
emotional expression is generally avoided, with indications of affection more 
commonly found on signet rings in scenes involving animals and their young.[3] 
Within this framework, the museum presentation of the Ring of Nestor emphasises
the composition of two couples—one at a building entrance and another on a 
shoreline marked by rocks and an oared ship—and the way in which the gestures 
of the left‑hand couple suggest a close relationship despite the abstraction of
their faces.[3] The museum reports that some interpreters have identified the 
couple, possibly shown in two successive moments, with Theseus and Ariadne in 
the course of their escape to Athens after the killing of the Minotaur, linking
the ship on the ring to the vessel imagery in the Flotilla Frieze from Akrotiri
on Thera dated to the 16th century BC.[3] Other interpreters, also cited by the
museum, regard the scene as a farewell between men departing for battle and 
their partners, comparable to the composition on the later Warrior Krater from 
Mycenae.[3]\n\nModern scholarship on the Ring of Nestor has likewise used it as
a case study in the assessment of authenticity and the history of 
archaeological reconstruction. The Metropolitan Museum of Art’s essay on 
historic images of the Greek Bronze Age discusses the role of Emile Gilliéron 
and his son as official artists for Greek museums and notes that their 
reconstructions at Knossos and other sites sometimes introduced elements not 
securely based on the archaeological record, such as the interpretation of the 
“Saffron‑Gatherer” fresco as a boy rather than a monkey and the addition of 
rock‑pattern borders to the “Bull‑Leapers” fresco.[4] The essay points out that
scholars have long debated these reconstructions and, in the case of the 
“Priest‑King” relief, have proposed multiple alternative compositions from the 
same fragments.[4] Against this background, studies by Ingo Pini and Theodore 
Eliopoulos use the Ring of Nestor to explore how stylistic comparison, 
technical observation and knowledge of early 20th‑century restoration practices
can be combined to distinguish authentic Bronze Age artefacts from possible 
forgeries in the Aegean corpus, with both authors arguing that the ring’s 
iconography and manufacture can be understood within Late Bronze Age religious 
and artistic traditions.[1][2][4][5]\n\n## References\n\n[1] (PDF) SOME 
