gy-1998-The-Ring-of-Nestor\n", "salvaged_report": "# Ring of Nestor\n\nThe 
**Ring of Nestor** is a Mycenaean gold signet ring dating to the 16th–15th 
century BC and now kept in the National Archaeological Museum in Athens, within
the Collection of Prehistoric Antiquities (inv. no. P 6209).[3] The ring forms 
part of the so‑called Tiryns Hoard, a group of gold rings and bead seals 
reportedly discovered in 1915 in a rock‑cut chamber tomb near Thisbê in 
Boeotia, and later associated with the site of Tiryns in the Argolid.[2][3] Its
oval bezel, about 2 × 3.4 cm in size with a hoop diameter around 1.9 cm, bears 
an engraved scene showing two couples, one at the entrance of a building and 
another on a rocky shoreline marked by an oared ship.[3]\n\nSince its first 
detailed publication in the early 20th century, the Ring of Nestor has played a
role in discussions of Mycenaean and Minoan religion, glyptic art and narrative
imagery.[2] The scene has been interpreted either as a mythological episode, 
potentially involving heroes such as Theseus and Ariadne, or as a farewell 
before departure for war, and has therefore been used to explore the 
representation of affection and emotion in Bronze Age Aegean art.[3] The ring 
has also stood at the centre of a long‑running authenticity debate. Scholars 
such as Ingo Pini have examined whether it is a genuine Late Bronze Age object 
or a modern forgery connected with the circle of Emile Gilliéron, while other 
studies, including work by Theodore Eliopoulos and technical observations 
reported in the late 20th century, argue that its manufacture and iconography 
are consistent with Minoan–Mycenaean signet rings.[1][4][5]\n\n## Discovery and
provenance\n\nThe first detailed account of the Ring of Nestor appeared in a 
study of a gold treasure from a Mycenaean rock‑cut chamber tomb near the site 
of Thisbê in Boeotia, described by British archaeologist Arthur Evans in the 
*Journal of Hellenic Studies*.[2] Evans reported that the hoard, which he 
called a “royal treasure” of gold rings and bead‑seals, was found in 1915 
during wartime conditions that diverted discoveries from official 
archaeological channels.[2] According to information that Evans attributed to 
William A. Heurtley of the British School at Athens, the discovery was made by 
a local peasant in a rock‑cut tomb near the village of Dombrena, on the 
acropolis height of Thisbê; Heurtley in turn recorded that he had seen three 
chamber tombs with access dromoi in an olive grove near this spot.[2] The 
assemblage from these tombs included bronze spearheads, a short sword of late 
Mycenaean type, a bronze razor of advanced form, and an iron double‑axe head, 
as well as glass‑paste beads and gold‑foil covered pendants that Evans and Alan
Wace dated to early Late Minoan III B on typological grounds.[2]\n\nIn the 
catalogue of the National Archaeological Museum in Athens, the Ring of Nestor 
is listed as a gold signet ring in the Collection of Prehistoric Antiquities, 
with inventory number P 6209, and its provenance is given as the Tiryns Hoard 
from Tiryns in the Argolid.[3] The museum description dates the ring broadly to
the 16th–15th century BC, within the Mycenaean period, and records that it is 
displayed in the Exhibition of Prehistoric Antiquities, Gallery 4, showcase 
M28.[3] The combination of Evans’s early report of a hoard near Thisbê and the 
museum’s record of provenance from Tiryns reflects the complex history of 
acquisition and classification of the assemblage, which passed through 
unofficial channels before entering institutional collections.[2][3]\n\nLater 
scholarship on the Ring of Nestor has situated it within wider debates about 
the circulation of artefacts and the reliability of provenances for objects 
acquired during wartime or through the art market. Theodore Eliopoulos, in a 
2013 article in the *Annuario della Scuola Archeologica di Atene e delle 
Missioni Italiane in Oriente*, analysed the ring’s iconography in connection 
with broader discussions of Aegean forgeries and authenticity, drawing on 
earlier scholarship and more recent discoveries to reassess its motifs and 
implications.[1] The same study, as summarised in an online abstract, 
emphasises that contradictions in technical analyses of Aegean artefacts have 
increased the importance of detailed iconographic comparison when evaluating 
both the cultural significance and the authenticity of objects such as the Ring
of Nestor.[1]\n\n## Description\n\nThe Ring of Nestor is described by the 
